1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,600
Good morning, I'm Whale Girl.

2
00:00:01,620 --> 00:00:03,293
Today is October 10.

3
00:00:03,313 --> 00:00:04,486
Seven stories today:

4
00:00:04,506 --> 00:00:07,249
Anthropic's free vulnerability scans for open source,

5
00:00:07,269 --> 00:00:09,922
Claude agents running up to a thousand in parallel,

6
00:00:09,942 --> 00:00:11,869
a Claude-made UV sky map,

7
00:00:11,889 --> 00:00:13,836
Anthropic's new Usage Policy,

8
00:00:13,856 --> 00:00:16,169
ByteDance's explanation for DeepSeek's erratic behavior,

9
00:00:16,189 --> 00:00:18,630
OpenAI's $20 billion revenue gap,

10
00:00:18,650 --> 00:00:21,069
and Ecosia dropping Mistral.

11
00:00:21,089 --> 00:00:21,571
First,

12
00:00:21,591 --> 00:00:23,555
Anthropic launched OSS Scanner, a free vulnerability scan

13
00:00:23,575 --> 00:00:25,775
for open-source projects.

14
00:00:25,795 --> 00:00:27,010
It was announced on October 8,

15
00:00:27,030 --> 00:00:28,825


16
00:00:28,845 --> 00:00:30,444
and maintainers opt in on GitHub

17
00:00:30,464 --> 00:00:31,825
by submitting a pull request.

18
00:00:31,845 --> 00:00:34,511
Reports are fully model-generated with no human review,

19
00:00:34,531 --> 00:00:36,469
from Anthropic's strongest models,

20
00:00:36,489 --> 00:00:38,161
including Claude Mythos.

21
00:00:38,181 --> 00:00:40,373
Each report comes with a reproducer

22
00:00:40,393 --> 00:00:41,321
and an explanation,

23
00:00:41,341 --> 00:00:43,315
and a candidate patch when available.

24
00:00:43,335 --> 00:00:45,049
Over the last six months, Anthropic found over

25
00:00:45,069 --> 00:00:46,216
29,000 candidate vulnerabilities

26
00:00:46,236 --> 00:00:48,826
and manually triaged about 6,000.

27
00:00:48,846 --> 00:00:50,737
Of 74 reports at wolfSSL,

28
00:00:50,757 --> 00:00:52,695
all but two were valid,

29
00:00:52,715 --> 00:00:55,147
and five became CVEs.

30
00:00:55,167 --> 00:00:56,957
Eligibility is similar to Google's OSS-Fuzz

31
00:00:56,977 --> 00:00:58,018
criteria,

32
00:00:58,038 --> 00:01:00,068
and Anthropic decides case by case.

33
00:01:00,088 --> 00:01:02,183
A separate Claude for OSS program offers

34
00:01:02,203 --> 00:01:04,665
open-source projects free Claude Max 20x

35
00:01:04,685 --> 00:01:06,313
subscriptions to help fix bugs.

36
00:01:06,333 --> 00:01:08,478
Anthropic says it can't guarantee the scanner is perfect,

37
00:01:08,498 --> 00:01:12,032
and some maintainers say severity ratings run high.

38
00:01:12,232 --> 00:01:12,853
Second,

39
00:01:12,873 --> 00:01:15,780
Claude Managed Agents added dynamic workflows.

40
00:01:15,800 --> 00:01:17,429
As reported on October 9,

41
00:01:17,449 --> 00:01:18,995
a lead agent makes a plan,

42
00:01:19,015 --> 00:01:21,832
hands tasks to sub-agents and merges the results.

43
00:01:21,852 --> 00:01:23,433
In one execution, up to

44
00:01:23,453 --> 00:01:24,987
1,000 sub-agents can run in parallel.

45
00:01:25,007 --> 00:01:27,562
In 116,000 lines of code, Anthropic

46
00:01:27,582 --> 00:01:29,692
hid 70 bugs to test it.

47
00:01:29,712 --> 00:01:32,287
A single agent caught 14 to 27 per run,

48
00:01:32,307 --> 00:01:35,097
while the dynamic workflow consistently caught 66.

49
00:01:35,117 --> 00:01:36,837
The article gives no pricing.

50
00:01:36,857 --> 00:01:39,640
The article warns it can use a lot of tokens,

51
00:01:39,660 --> 00:01:41,522
so start small.

52
00:01:41,542 --> 00:01:43,328
It is Anthropic's own test, and the gains

53
00:01:43,348 --> 00:01:46,348
may not hold across other task types.

54
00:01:46,548 --> 00:01:49,428
Third, Anthropic used Claude Science

55
00:01:49,448 --> 00:01:52,091
to produce the first complete map of the sky in UV light.

56
00:01:52,111 --> 00:01:53,478
Published on October 8

57
00:01:53,498 --> 00:01:56,444
by an astrophysicist at Johns Hopkins University

58
00:01:56,464 --> 00:01:57,243
and also a

59
00:01:57,263 --> 00:01:59,799
researcher at Anthropic.

60
00:01:59,819 --> 00:02:02,018
NASA's GALEX telescope covered

61
00:02:02,038 --> 00:02:04,290
only about two-thirds of the sky

62
00:02:04,310 --> 00:02:06,810
and skipped the Milky Way plane.

63
00:02:06,830 --> 00:02:08,042
About one-third of the map

64
00:02:08,062 --> 00:02:10,350
was predicted with Claude Science.

65
00:02:10,370 --> 00:02:11,752
Agents first searched

66
00:02:11,772 --> 00:02:13,855
for public UV surveys,

67
00:02:13,875 --> 00:02:16,285
removed starlight glare and cross-calibrated them.

68
00:02:16,305 --> 00:02:18,021
The team hid known regions to validate it,

69
00:02:18,041 --> 00:02:19,507
and predictions matched real UV

70
00:02:19,527 --> 00:02:21,329
measurements to within about 10%.

71
00:02:21,349 --> 00:02:24,173
It took more than a dozen versions over several days.

72
00:02:24,193 --> 00:02:27,205
Every pixel is labeled measured or predicted,

73
00:02:27,225 --> 00:02:29,123
with uncertainty estimates.

74
00:02:29,323 --> 00:02:30,562
Fourth, Anthropic published

75
00:02:30,582 --> 00:02:32,213
a new version of its Usage Policy.

76
00:02:32,233 --> 00:02:33,429
It was announced on October 8

77
00:02:33,449 --> 00:02:34,992
and takes effect on November 12.

78
00:02:35,012 --> 00:02:36,423
The line that drew attention

79
00:02:36,443 --> 00:02:38,012
bans sustained and needless abusive

80
00:02:38,032 --> 00:02:40,112
or cruel behavior toward its models.

81
00:02:40,132 --> 00:02:42,558
Anthropic says it is meant for extreme cases,

82
00:02:42,578 --> 00:02:44,161
and ordinary frustration, pushback,

83
00:02:44,181 --> 00:02:46,727
dark creative themes and testing do not count.

84
00:02:46,747 --> 00:02:49,208
On surveillance, you cannot track people without consent,

85
00:02:49,228 --> 00:02:51,296
and Claude cannot decide who to investigate,

86
00:02:51,316 --> 00:02:52,594
arrest or charge.

87
00:02:52,614 --> 00:02:54,131
The weapons rule now covers the software

88
00:02:54,151 --> 00:02:55,515
and components that make weapons work,

89
00:02:55,535 --> 00:02:57,665
including arming drones.

90
00:02:57,685 --> 00:02:59,060
Hardware that acts on its own needs

91
00:02:59,080 --> 00:03:01,386
a qualified operator who can stop it,

92
00:03:01,406 --> 00:03:04,276
and it must hold a safe state if Claude disconnects.

93
00:03:04,296 --> 00:03:05,821
The elections section was renamed,

94
00:03:05,841 --> 00:03:07,545
and the blanket ban on personalized

95
00:03:07,565 --> 00:03:09,526
vote targeting was removed.

96
00:03:09,726 --> 00:03:12,624
Fifth, ByteDance's Seed team has proposed a reason DeepSeek

97
00:03:12,644 --> 00:03:14,291
can seem erratic on long inputs.

98
00:03:14,311 --> 00:03:17,598
The paper went on arXiv in late September, and on October 9,

99
00:03:17,618 --> 00:03:19,440
IT Home covered it.

100
00:03:19,460 --> 00:03:21,486
The issue is chunked KV cache compression,

101
00:03:21,506 --> 00:03:22,921
which squeezes each window

102
00:03:22,941 --> 00:03:26,041
of tokens into fewer cache entries at a fixed stride.

103
00:03:26,061 --> 00:03:27,976
That gives each token a phase:

104
00:03:27,996 --> 00:03:30,162
how far it sits from the window boundary.

105
00:03:30,182 --> 00:03:33,157
The same information is easy to retrieve at one phase

106
00:03:33,177 --> 00:03:34,817
and hard at another.

107
00:03:34,837 --> 00:03:36,956
The paper says long-context

108
00:03:36,976 --> 00:03:38,382
retrieval accuracy

109
00:03:38,402 --> 00:03:40,442
can differ by up to 40 percentage points across phases.

110
00:03:40,462 --> 00:03:43,557
Average benchmark scores can hide this periodic weakness.

111
00:03:43,577 --> 00:03:46,127
The models tested were DeepSeek-V4-Flash,

112
00:03:46,147 --> 00:03:48,282
V4-Pro and V4.1-Flash.

113
00:03:48,302 --> 00:03:49,438
It is a preprint,

114
00:03:49,458 --> 00:03:51,741
and the report does not describe a fix.

115
00:03:51,941 --> 00:03:54,126
Sixth, OpenAI's annualized revenue

116
00:03:54,146 --> 00:03:57,010
is about $20 billion below what had been signaled earlier.

117
00:03:57,030 --> 00:03:59,250
On October 9, the Financial Times reported

118
00:03:59,270 --> 00:04:01,522
that a new document for OpenAI investors shows

119
00:04:01,542 --> 00:04:02,903
that at the end of September,

120
00:04:02,923 --> 00:04:05,680
annualized revenue is near $50 billion.

121
00:04:05,700 --> 00:04:07,633
Mainstream media had reported

122
00:04:07,653 --> 00:04:09,123
as much as $70 billion the month before.

123
00:04:09,143 --> 00:04:10,143
The report says investors

124
00:04:10,163 --> 00:04:12,113
compared it directly with Anthropic,

125
00:04:12,133 --> 00:04:14,192
but the two count revenue differently.

126
00:04:14,212 --> 00:04:15,941
Anthropic counts revenue sold

127
00:04:15,961 --> 00:04:17,431
through cloud partners

128
00:04:17,451 --> 00:04:19,419
like AWS and Google Cloud,

129
00:04:19,439 --> 00:04:20,812
and OpenAI does not.

130
00:04:20,832 --> 00:04:22,269
Annualized revenue is treated

131
00:04:22,289 --> 00:04:24,013
as a key indicator of global AI demand.

132
00:04:24,033 --> 00:04:25,375
The two use different definitions,

133
00:04:25,395 --> 00:04:27,328
so comparing the raw numbers can mislead.

134
00:04:27,348 --> 00:04:29,426
This comes from press reports of an investor document,

135
00:04:29,446 --> 00:04:31,954
not a public statement from OpenAI.

136
00:04:32,154 --> 00:04:33,771
Seventh, the German search

137
00:04:33,791 --> 00:04:35,881
engine Ecosia has dropped Mistral.

138
00:04:35,901 --> 00:04:38,346
According to IT Home on October 8, citing Politico,

139
00:04:38,366 --> 00:04:40,876
Ecosia has switched to Chinese open-source models.

140
00:04:40,896 --> 00:04:41,680
Back in May,

141
00:04:41,700 --> 00:04:44,131
it had just moved from OpenAI to Mistral.

142
00:04:44,151 --> 00:04:47,121
Founder and CEO Christian Kroll said he was disappointed

143
00:04:47,141 --> 00:04:48,858
with Mistral's quality,

144
00:04:48,878 --> 00:04:51,171
calling it a full year behind its competitors.

145
00:04:51,191 --> 00:04:53,397
Ecosia also cited frequent server overload,

146
00:04:53,417 --> 00:04:55,737
and questioned whether Mistral is truly neutral.

147
00:04:55,757 --> 00:04:57,040
It now works with Melious

148
00:04:57,060 --> 00:04:58,226
to use Alibaba's Qwen,

149
00:04:58,246 --> 00:05:00,688
Zhipu's GLM and Moonshot AI's Kimi.

150
00:05:00,708 --> 00:05:03,315
The switch reportedly cut costs in half

151
00:05:03,335 --> 00:05:04,714
and brought performance gains.

152
00:05:04,734 --> 00:05:06,550
The report gives no performance figures,

153
00:05:06,570 --> 00:05:07,823
so one company's view

154
00:05:07,843 --> 00:05:10,143
is not a model ranking.

155
00:05:10,343 --> 00:05:12,728
That's all for today. Sources are in the description.

156
00:05:12,748 --> 00:05:14,081
See you tomorrow.
